cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use pyardl in your work, please cite the software as below, as
  well as the underlying methodological articles: Pesaran, Shin & Smith
  (2001) for the bounds test, Kripfganz & Schneider (2020) for the
  response surfaces and p-values, and Narayan (2005) for the
  small-sample bounds. Sources for every shipped critical value are
  documented in src/pyardl/critical_values/PROVENANCE.md, and the
  conventions settled by measurement rather than by reading are recorded
  in docs/VALIDATION_OBSERVATIONS.md.
type: software
title: "pyardl: ARDL models and bounds-test cointegration in Python"
authors:
  - family-names: Adjoh
    given-names: Komlan Matthania
    email: komlanadjohmatthania@gmail.com
version: 0.4.0
date-released: "2026-08-22"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Matth-analyst/pyardl"
keywords:
  - econometrics
  - time series
  - ARDL
  - cointegration
  - bounds test
  - error correction model
  - NARDL
  - bootstrap
abstract: >-
  A Python time-series econometrics library for ARDL models: ARDL and
  UECM estimation, Pesaran-Shin-Smith bounds tests for cointegration
  (five deterministic cases, joint F and t decision), multi-source
  critical values with documented provenance (Pesaran, Shin & Smith
  2001 tables, Narayan 2005 small-sample bounds, Kripfganz-Schneider
  response surfaces with p-values), the three-test framework of Sam,
  McNown & Goh (2019) that names both degeneracies, bootstrap critical
  values with no inconclusive zone, the nonlinear ARDL of Shin, Yu &
  Greenwood-Nimmo (2014) with its asymmetric dynamic multipliers, and a
  reproducible Monte Carlo simulation engine.
